<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dang Hyang Nirartha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Pedanda Shakti Wawu Rauh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dang Hyang Nirartha was a Shaivite priest from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> in the 16th century, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalem-waturenggong">Dalem Waturenggong</a> of Gelgel. He reformed Balinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, introducing the padmasana shrine to the supreme god, and is credited with founding sea temples such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanah-lot">Tanah Lot</a> and Uluwatu. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> families of Bali trace their descent from him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dang-hyang-nirartha.jpg" alt="Dang Hyang Nirartha" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schweigeblick · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dang_Hyang_Nirartha_Statue_Uluwatu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dang Hyang Nirartha, also known as Ida Pedanda Sakti Wawu Rauh, Mpu Nirartha and Dang Hyang Dwijendra, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> during the final period of the Majapahit Kingdom and spent most of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. He came from an elite social milieu and was a disciple of Syekh Siti Jenar, a Javanese member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Sanga</a>, the revered Muslim saints of Java, who taught a mystical, pantheist form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> opposed to the shariatic Sufism of Sunan Kudus. Balinese texts call him a poet, intellectual, wonderworker and advisor to rulers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> by the Javanese royal court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> and became Bagawanta, the royal priest, of the Gelgel Kingdom. His coming brought the support of the spiritual world (niskala) and the sanction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>'s earthly powers to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalem-waturenggong">Dalem Waturenggong</a>'s rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasuruan">Pasuruan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blambangan">Blambangan</a>, Puger, Bali, Nusa Penida, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasak-people">Sasak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a>, and he was to choose a local priest to lead the rajasurya or aswameda ceremony the king held for the occasion.</p><p>His travels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a> are recounted in a lontara called Dwijendra Tatwa. He was still alive in Bali in 1537: a colophon he wrote on a copy of the kakavin Sumanasāntaka records that the copy was completed on 14 July 1537. According to the Dwijendratattwa and the Babad Brāhmaṇa, he had completed a work called Mahiṣa Mĕgat Kūng the year before.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Nirartha refashioned Balinese Hinduism and promoted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> the idea of moksha, freedom from the cycle of death and rebirth. He founded the Shaivite priesthood now found throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> and is regarded as the ancestor of all Shaivite pedandas. The padmasana shrine he introduced, an empty throne serving as an altar to the supreme god Acintya or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, was added by the dozen to the coastal temples of Bali during his travels.</p><p>A tradition among the brahmana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> holds that wetu telu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, a mix of Islam and animism, was brought to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasak-people">Sasaks</a> as his teaching, and that he took the name Pangèran Sangupati in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> and Tuan Seméru in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a>; another version makes Pangèran Sangupati a different person, perhaps a Sasak pupil of his. Legend says he crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> on a pumpkin, the origin of a taboo on eating pumpkins among some Balinese Brahmins, and that a hair he gave the king to end a plague was placed in a temple that became a Shaivite pilgrimage site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dang Hyang Nirartha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Javanese priest <b>Dang Hyang Nirartha</b>, who came to Bali in that era, reshaped Balinese Hinduism and founded temples such as Tanah Lot and Uluwatu.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gelgel">Kingdom of Gelgel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in Bali Island, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.31,109.42&amp;year=1564" aria-label="Java on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7578 2437 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dang Hyang Nirartha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8028 2707h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8028 2707h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.31,109.42&amp;year=1564">What was here in 1564? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
